  "query_preview": "..\n\nActive southwesterly flow sits over southeastern Arizona today\namid broad eastern Pacific troughing. In response, lower level\nsoutherly flow was gradually drawing moisture northward into far\neastern areas this morning, as depicted by the GOES satellite\nderived precipitable water product. By this afternoon\nprecipitable water values near the New Mexico border should be \nnearing 1 inch, which will be adequate for 10-20 percent chances\nof showers/thunderstorms through the early evening near the\nborder.\n\nTonight, a sub-tropical jet associated with the Pacific trough\nwill push over northern Sonora, putting southeastern Arizona\nunder the left exit region of the jet. This favorable \norientation will drive an unseasonably rich moisture plume \nnorthward through Friday across the region with precipitable \nwater values of 1-1.4 inches and surface dew points in the 50s \nto lower 60s becoming common. Increased cloudiness in the \nmorning is expected with this moisture push along with a chance\nof isolated showers and thunderstorms, becoming more likely in \nthe afternoon. The early potential convection and cloudiness \nmakes the afternoon/evening thunderstorm strength more \nuncertain, as instability may be limited. That said, ample \nforcing aloft combined with strong mid-level flow will provide a\nrisk for strong and organized thunderstorms, capable of heavy \nrainfall rates, damaging wind gusts, and hail. With storm \nmotions likely to be fairly quick, any flash flooding threat \nwill likely be kept to areas that see multiple rounds of \nthunderstorms. 00Z HREF/REFS ensemble members highlight an area \nof central Arizona, mainly from northern Pinal to the north, \nwhere excessive rainfall with 1-2+ inch probabilities is \npossible through Friday evening. There remains some time for \nthis to shift and a flash flooding risk can't be ruled out for \nany area of southeastern Arizona.\n\nOn Saturday the subtropical jet will lift north through Arizona\nwith mid-level height rises keeping the forecast mainly on the \ndry side through the day. Lingering moisture may produce an \nisolated shower in the afternoon. The drier forecast will be \nshort-lived however as the eastern Pacific trough is expected to\ntighten and move into the western US Saturday night and Sunday.\nThis potent wave will be associated with an impressive moisture\nfeed with precipitable water values increasing to 1-1.5 inches,\nnear maxing out the NAEFS climatology for this time of year. \nChances for showers and thunderstorms will first increase \nSaturday night, though there remains some discrepancies in how \nearly this activity will develop. An initial round of isolated \nto scattered showers/thunderstorms will be possible in the \nevening. The main wave will move in during the day Sunday with \nchances increasing from west to east. Though instability will be\nmore limited, long duration moderate to locally heavy rainfall \nrates may bring a threat of rapid runoff and localized flash \nflooding. Rainfall probabilities remain generally consistent \nwith previous shifts with around 0.5-1 inches in the valleys and\n1-2 inches in the mountains forecast. Areas of Pinal, Graham, \nand Greenlee counties have chances for even greater totals with \n10-40 percent chances of exceeding 2 inches through early Monday\nmorning. With strong southwesterly integrated water transport \nassociated with this wave, southwestern slopes from the \nCatalina/Rincons north through the White Mountains have a low \nbut non-zero chance of seeing rainfall totals pushing 3 inches. \nIf these high end amounts are realized, increased flows in \nrivers of Graham and Greenlee counties may be seen with any\nimpacts lingering into early next week.\n\nThough the bulk of the heaviest precipitation will be exiting by\nlate Sunday night, shower and thunderstorm chances will last\nthrough Monday morning with a crossing front associated with the\nupper level wave. Temperatures both Sunday and Monday are \nforecast to fall around 5-10 degrees below normal, potentially \neven cooler on Sunday in areas that see rainfall through the \nafternoon. Temperatures will then likely moderate to near or \njust below normal by by mid- week after the system fully exits \nto the east.",
